<!--
  Litestone page § A row's whole life. Claims lifted from
  packages/litestone/docs/soft-delete.md (cascade, restore matches the cascade's
  stamp), trail.md (@@trail entries, before/after, the trash entry),
  schema.md and README.md § @version (the 409), sequences.md (per-scope counter,
  written at create) and traits.md (@@trait splices a cluster in).
  The proof is a real run, not a doc fence: the schema below was loaded with
  createClient and the calls in OUT were made against it as asSystem(), because a
  trail database puts every model behind an access rule. Values are quoted from
  that run; the order and layout are abridged.
  Not claimed: the cascade writes no trail entry of its own for the children (the
  run showed one Account delete and none for the invoices), a soft delete does not
  bump @version (the run showed 1 before and after), and a trait cannot carry a
  second @@softDelete, so the trait holds the column and the model names the
  attribute.
  The samples sit in consts: a brace in markup opens a Mesa expression.
-->
<script>
  const SCHEMA = `database audit { path "./audit/"  driver trail }

trait Life {
  version   Int       @version
  deletedAt DateTime?
  @@trail(audit)
}

model Account {
  id       Int @id
  name     String
  invoices Invoice[]
  @@trait(Life)
  @@softDelete(cascade)
}

model Invoice {
  id        Int @id
  accountId Int
  account   Account @relation(fields: [accountId], references: [id])
  number    Int     @sequence(scope: accountId)
  amount    Int
  @@trait(Life)
  @@softDelete
}`

  const OUT = `// three invoices: two for account 1, one for account 2
number    1, 2, 1        version 1

// Alice and Bob both read invoice 1 at version 1. Alice saves.
update { amount: 120, version: 1 }   ->  version: 2

// Bob saves from the copy he read.
update { amount: 90, version: 1 }
VersionConflictError  409, retryable
  Version conflict on Invoice: expected version 1, row is at 2 -
  it was modified after you read it

// delete the account
account.remove({ where: { id: 1 } })
invoice.count()                       ->  1   live
invoice.count({ withDeleted: true })  ->  3
invoice.findMany({ onlyDeleted: true })
  -> ids 1 and 2, deletedAt "2026-11-03T05:24:19.209Z" on both

// the trail, one entry for the account
operation delete   model Account   records [1]
before { version: 1, deletedAt: null, id: 1, name: "acme" }
after  { version: 1, deletedAt: "2026-11-03T05:24:19.209Z", ... }

account.restore({ where: { id: 1 } })
invoice.count()                       ->  3`
</script>

<section id="life" class="bc-section">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">A row's whole life</p>
    <h2>The delete flag, the audit table and the version check are already written.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Declare what should happen to a row when it is deleted, edited twice at once or
      numbered, and the engine does it on every write path. Put the shared columns in a
      <code>trait</code> once and every model that carries it gets them.
    </p>

    <div class="bc-split">
      <ul class="bc-points">
        <li>
          <strong>Delete takes the children and gives them back.</strong>
          <code>@@softDelete(cascade)</code> stamps every child with the parent's own timestamp,
          and <code>restore()</code> returns the children that stamp marked, leaving one deleted
          earlier on its own still deleted.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The history writes itself.</strong>
          <code>@@trail(audit)</code> logs every create, update and delete with the row before
          and after, and a field marked <code>@secret</code> or <code>@encrypted</code> is
          logged as <code>[redacted]</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A stale save is a 409, not a lost edit.</strong>
          A <code>@version</code> column is checked in the update's own <code>WHERE</code>, so
          the second writer gets a retryable <code>VersionConflictError</code> instead of
          silently overwriting the first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Per-account numbers that cannot collide.</strong>
          <code>@sequence(scope: accountId)</code> draws the next number inside the write
          transaction, and an update that tries to change it is refused.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="bc-proof">
        <pre class="code">{SCHEMA}</pre>
        <pre class="code">{OUT}</pre>
        <figcaption>
          A real run against that schema with the package's own client, as
          <code>asSystem()</code>. Abridged: the calls are shortened and the trail row is
          cut to the fields that matter. The cascade wrote no trail entry of its own for the
          invoices, and a soft delete leaves <code>version</code> where it was.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="bc-more">
      Read more:
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/soft-delete.md">soft delete and cascade</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/trail.md">the audit trail</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/schema.md">@version</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/sequences.md">sequences</a> ·
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/litestone/docs/traits.md">traits</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .bc-proof pre.code { overflow-x: auto; font-size: .82rem; margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
</style>
